window.docContents[144].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Section-2___12___-The-tensor-algebra-___-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___12_heading":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.12. The tensor algebra\u0009Section 2.12: The tensor algebra — heading and introduction","header":"Section 2.12: The tensor algebra — heading and introduction","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Section-2___12___-The-tensor-algebra-___-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___12_heading"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The induced scalar action agrees with the given action of the corresponding opposite-ring element.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Remark2.3.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Left and right modules over commutative rings\u0009Left and right modules over commutative rings\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings--Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_--R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Lie equivalence between three-dimensional real vectors and the orthogonal Lie subalgebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Exercise2.9.5; role=supporting]\n\nThere exists a Lie equivalence from the orthogonal Lie subalgebra to three-dimensional real vectors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Exercise2.9.5; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009R^3 cross product as special case of so(3)\u0009R^3 cross product as special case of so(3)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_--R___3-cross-product-as-special-case-of-so_LPAR_3_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_3/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Irreducible representations of S_3\u0009Irreducible representations of S_3","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_3/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S3___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___3":{"contents":"Remark 4.5.3. We see that characters of irreducible complex representations of G can be defined without mentioning irreducible representations. Namely, equip the space F(G, \\mathbb{C}) of complex-valued functions on G with the convolution product\n\n(f * g)(z) = \\sum_{x, y \\in G : xy = z} f(x)g(y).\n\nThis product turns F(G, \\mathbb{C}) into an associative algebra, with unit \\delta_e (the characteristic function of the unit e \\in G), and the space of class functions F_c(G, \\mathbb{C}) is a commutative subalgebra. Then one can define renormalized characters \\tilde{\\chi}_i \\in F_c(G, \\mathbb{C}) to be the primitive idempotents in this algebra, i.e., solutions of the equation f * f = f which cannot be decomposed into a sum of other nonzero solutions. Then one can define the characters by the formula\n\n\\chi_i(g) = \\sqrt{\\frac{|G|}{\\tilde{\\chi}_i(1)}} \\tilde{\\chi}_i(g)\n\n(check it!). This is, essentially, how Frobenius defined characters (see [Cu], equation (7)). Note that Frobenius defined representations at approximately the same time, but for some time it was not clear that there is a simple relation between irreducible representations and characters (namely, that irreducible characters are simply traces of group elements in irreducible representations). Even today, many group theorists sometimes talk of irreducible characters of a finite group rather than irreducible representations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Characters defined without representations via convolution algebra","header":"Characters defined without representations via convolution algebra","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___3"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Section-4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters-___-Hermitian-inner-product-on-class-functions/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___5":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Section 4.5: Orthogonality of characters — Hermitian inner product on class functions","header":"Section 4.5: Orthogonality of characters — Hermitian inner product on class functions","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Section-4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters-___-Hermitian-inner-product-on-class-functions/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___5"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Frobenius-character-formula-for-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___15___1":{"contents":"Theorem 5.15.1. Let N \\geq p. Then \\chi_{V_\\lambda}(C_\\mathbf{i}) is the coefficient of x^{\\lambda+\\rho} := \\prod x_j^{\\lambda_j + N - j} in the polynomial\n\n\\Delta(x) \\prod_{m \\geq 1} H_m(x)^{i_m}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Frobenius character formula for Specht modules V_lambda","header":"Frobenius character formula for Specht modules V_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Frobenius-character-formula-for-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___15___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/Section-5___20___-Historical-interlude-___-Hermann-Weyl/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___20___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.20. Historical interlude: Hermann Weyl at the intersection of limitation and freedom\u0009Section 5.20: Historical interlude — Hermann Weyl\u0009Section 5.20: Historical interlude — Hermann Weyl","header":"5.20. Historical interlude: Hermann Weyl at the intersection of limitation and freedom","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/Section-5___20___-Historical-interlude-___-Hermann-Weyl/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___20___heading-1"}});